imary amenorrhea in prepubertal patients. \n\nAnorexia nervosa raises complex medical and ethical issues, particularly regarding treatment and patient autonomy, especially in detention settings. It can lead to severe health risks, necessitating nutritional rehabilitation and, in some cases, enteral nutrition support. The disorder is also associated with physiological conditions such as hypercortisolism, although it rarely presents with clinical features of Cushing's syndrome. Overall, anorexia nervosa is a multifaceted mental health disorder that poses significant challenges for both patients and healthcare providers.\",49\r\n8898,GASTROPARESIA,\"Gastroparesis is a medical condition that affects the stomach muscles, preventing proper stomach emptying. It is often observed in diabetic patients and can lead to significant weight loss, particularly in cases such as anorexia nervosa. The condition is characterized by delayed gastric emptying, which can result in increased gastric residue, especially in patients receiving enteral nutrition. Improvement in gastroparesis symptoms is typically achieved through nutritional rehabilitation and dietary adjustments.\",4\r\n4173,LEPTIN,\"Leptin is a hormone produced by adipose (fat) tissue that plays a crucial role in regulating energy balance and metabolism. It influences insulin sensitivity and appetite, particularly during pregnancy. Research has shown that leptin levels are significantly altered in patients with anorexia nervosa, where low levels of the hormone correlate with reduced body weight. This indicates that leptin is not only important for general energy regulation but also has specific implications for individuals with eating disorders, such as anorexia nervosa.\",3\r\n8752,TC,\"Tomografía Computarizada (TC) is a medical imaging technique utilized to visualize internal structures of the body. It plays a crucial role in diagnosing various medical conditions, including those related to anorexia and bulimia. However, the use of TC can be complicated in patients with morbid obesity due to their size, which may pose challenges in obtaining clear images.\",2\r\n8753,RM,\"La resonancia magnética (RM) es una técnica de imagen médica que proporciona imágenes detalladas de órganos y tejidos, siendo especialmente útil para evaluar cambios neurológicos en pacientes con trastornos alimentarios. Esta prueba diagnóstica puede ser difícil de realizar en pacientes con obesidad mórbida, lo que puede complicar su uso en esta población. La RM se utiliza para evaluar la salud de los tejidos blandos y del cerebro en pacientes que padecen trastornos alimentarios, lo que resalta su importancia en el diagnóstico y seguimiento de estas condiciones.\",2\r\n8911,EDUCATIONAL INSTITUTION,An institution that may provide psychological and nutritional education to patients recovering from eating disorders.,2\r\n8921,GHRELIN,\"Ghrelin is a hormone that plays a significant role in regulating appetite and influencing various hormonal releases, particularly in patients with anorexia nervosa. Research has shown that ghrelin levels are elevated in individuals suffering from anorexia nervosa, indicating a state of negative energy balance. This elevation suggests that ghrelin may be involved in the physiological responses associated with this eating disorder, highlighting its importance in understanding appetite regulation and energy homeostasis in affected patients.\",2\r\n3758,HYPOTHALAMUS,\"The hypothalamus is a critical region of the brain that plays a vital role in controlling the pituitary gland and regulating various hormonal functions within the body. It is integral to the endocrine system, influencing the secretion of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) from the pituitary gland. Additionally, the hypothalamus is involved in the dysregulation that can lead to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, particularly in patients suffering from Anorexia Nervosa. This highlights its importance not only in general hormone regulation but also in specific medical conditions related to hormonal imbalances.\",2\r\n8913,AMENORREA,\"Amenorrhea is defined as the absence of menstruation and can manifest in two forms: primary and secondary. This condition is often observed in individuals with Anorexia Nervosa, where it is primarily linked to hormonal imbalances. In patients suffering from Anorexia Nervosa, the disruption of normal hormonal function can lead to the cessation of menstrual cycles, highlighting the significant impact of this eating disorder on reproductive health.\",1\r\n4965,DSM-IV,\"The DSM-IV is a manual published by the American Psychiatric Association that outlines the criteria for diagnosing mental disorders, including anorexia nerviosa.\",1\r\n8880,MODELOS,Grupo profesional que parece estar más afectado por trastornos de la conducta alimentaria debido a presiones sociales sobre la imagen corporal.,1\r\n8882,GIMNASTAS,\"Grupo profesional que se encuentra en riesgo elevado de desarrollar trastornos de la conducta alimentaria, similar a modelos y atletas.\",1\r\n8886,FACTORES DE MAL PRONÓSTICO,\"Los factores de mal pronóstico en los trastornos de la conducta alimentaria incluyen vómitos, conductas purgativas y pérdida de peso importante.\",1\r\n8887,FACTORES DE BUEN PRONÓSTICO,Los factores de buen pronóstico incluyen el inicio en adolescentes jóvenes y una duración corta de la enfermedad.,1\r\n8922,ESTROGENS,Estrogens are hormones whose production decreases in patients with Anorexia Nerviosa due to loss of weight and fat mass.,1\r\n8923,MENSTRUATION,Menstruation recovery is an important indicator of healing in patients with Anorexia Nerviosa.,1\r\n8924,RT3,\"Reverse T3 (rT3) is a thyroid hormone that may be elevated in patients with Anorexia Nerviosa, indicating metabolic adaptation to malnutrition.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n3529,PACIENTE,ANOREXIA NERVIOSA,\"The patient is diagnosed with anorexia nerviosa, a 